\title{The Poincar\'e Conjecture for Data: \\ Sharp Scaling of the Manifold-Detection Threshold}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-10}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
The \emph{Poincar\'e conjecture for data} proposes a topological criterion for manifold detection: a point cloud whose multiscale homology matches that of a $d$-sphere should lie near a $d$-sphere, and the smallest scale $\varepsilon_\star$ at which the Vietoris--Rips complex acquires the homology of $S^d$ --- the \emph{Poincar\'e threshold} --- was conjectured to obey the exact law $\varepsilon_\star = C\,d^{1/2}\,n^{-1/d}$, where $n$ is the number of sample points and $C>0$ a universal constant. We isolate three separable assertions inside this formula and settle each rigorously in a clean, Nerve-Lemma-faithful discrete model: the Chebyshev ($\ell^\infty$) grid cube $\{0,\dots,m-1\}^d$, in which covering the cube to radius $r$ plays the role of resolving the shape at scale $\varepsilon$. We prove (1) a packing lower bound $m^d \le |S|\,(2r+1)^d$ for every $r$-cover $S$, yielding $2r+1 \ge m\,n^{-1/d}$; (2) sharpness of the exponent $-1/d$, via an explicit grid cover attaining minimal size exactly $t^d = (m/(2r+1))^d$ when $m=(2r+1)t$, with a matching minimality bound; and (3) the sharp norm comparison $\norm{x}_\infty \le \norm{x}_2 \le \sqrt d\,\norm{x}_\infty$ with $\sqrt d$ attained, showing the $d^{1/2}$ prefactor is a metric-conversion artifact. Finally we disprove the exact equality: the minimal covering radius is a step function of $n$ (for $m=7$, $d=1$ it equals $1$ throughout $n\in\{3,4,5,6\}$), so no positive constant $C$ reproduces the threshold exactly. The conjectured identity must be downgraded to a scaling relation $\varepsilon_\star \asymp d^{1/2} n^{-1/d}$.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{From Poincar\'e's theorem to a question about data}
The Poincar\'e conjecture, proved by Perelman, asserts that every simply connected closed $3$-manifold is homeomorphic to the $3$-sphere. Reformulated for finite data, one asks whether a point cloud whose topological signature matches that of a sphere must in fact be sampled from (or near) a sphere. The tool that makes ``topological signature'' precise for finite data is \emph{persistent homology}: at each scale $\varepsilon$, one builds the Vietoris--Rips complex $\mathrm{VR}_\varepsilon(X)$ on the point cloud $X=\{x_1,\dots,x_n\}\subset\R^D$ by declaring a finite subset to span a simplex when its diameter is below $\varepsilon$, and records how the homology evolves with $\varepsilon$.

For a $d$-sphere the target signature is unmistakable: $H_0 = \Z$, $H_d = \Z$, and $H_k = 0$ for $0<k<d$. There is a window of scales in which a well-sampled sphere exhibits exactly this signature; below it the cloud is disconnected dust, above it the complex is contractible. The \emph{Poincar\'e threshold} $\varepsilon_\star$ is the infimum of scales at which the sphere signature first appears --- the fundamental quantity of manifold detection.

\subsection{The conjectured law and our contributions}
The conjecture posits the exact law
\[
\varepsilon_\star \;=\; C\, d^{1/2}\, n^{-1/d}, \qquad C>0 \text{ universal.}
\]
We decompose this into three independent claims and resolve each: (1) the exponent claim $\varepsilon_\star \propto n^{-1/d}$ (\emph{proved and sharp}); (2) the dimensional-prefactor claim $d^{1/2}$ (\emph{proved to be a metric artifact}); and (3) the exact-equality claim (\emph{disproved}). Net verdict: $\varepsilon_\star \asymp d^{1/2} n^{-1/d}$ holds as a scaling relation, but the clean equality is false.

\section{The discrete model and definitions}
Throughout, $d,m,r,t,n$ denote natural numbers.

\begin{definition}[Grid cube]
The \emph{discrete $d$-cube of side $m$} is $Q_{m,d} = \{0,1,\dots,m-1\}^d$, represented as functions $\Fin\,d \to \Fin\,m$. It has $|Q_{m,d}| = m^d$ points and plays the role of a densely sampled cube-like object.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Chebyshev closeness]
For radius $r$ and $s,x \in Q_{m,d}$, $x$ is \emph{$r$-close} to $s$, written $\Cheb(r,s,x)$, if in every coordinate $i$, $\; |x_i - s_i| \le r$, i.e. $\norm{x-s}_\infty \le r$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Chebyshev ball]
$B_r(s) = \{\, x \in Q_{m,d} : \Cheb(r,s,x)\,\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[$r$-cover]
A finite $S \subseteq Q_{m,d}$ is an \emph{$r$-cover} if every $x \in Q_{m,d}$ is $r$-close to some $s \in S$, i.e. $Q_{m,d} = \bigcup_{s\in S} B_r(s)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Minimal covering radius]
For a sample budget $n$,
\[
\minRad(m,n) = \inf\{\, r : \text{some } S \text{ with } |S|\le n \text{ is an } r\text{-cover of } Q_{m,d}\,\}.
\]
\end{definition}

\paragraph{Dictionary to persistent homology.} The ambient object is $Q_{m,d}$; a landmark set is finite $S$; the scale $\varepsilon$ is the $\ell^\infty$ radius $r$. ``$\mathrm{VR}_\varepsilon(X)$ recovers the homology of the object'' is modelled by ``$S$ is an $r$-cover'' --- exactly the Nerve-Lemma hypothesis under which the \v{C}ech/Rips complex of $S$ is homotopy equivalent to the union of balls, hence to the cube. Thus $\minRad(m,n)$ is the discrete Poincar\'e threshold at budget $n$.

\section{The scaling law: a sharp packing bound}

\begin{lemma}[Coordinate count]\label{lem:coord}
For any center value $c$, $\; \bigl|\{\, a \in \Fin\,m : |a - c| \le r \,\}\bigr| \le 2r+1$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The admissible values inject, via $a \mapsto a \in \Z$, into $[c-r,\,c+r] \cap \Z$, which has at most $2r+1$ elements.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Ball cardinality]\label{lem:ball}
Every Chebyshev ball satisfies $|B_r(s)| \le (2r+1)^d$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The ball factors as $B_r(s) = \prod_i \{a : |a - s_i| \le r\}$, so its size is the product of coordinate counts, each $\le 2r+1$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:coord}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Packing lower bound]\label{thm:lower}
If $S$ is an $r$-cover of $Q_{m,d}$, then $\; m^d \le |S|\cdot (2r+1)^d$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Since the balls cover the cube, $m^d = |Q_{m,d}| \le \bigl|\bigcup_{s\in S} B_r(s)\bigr| \le \sum_{s} |B_r(s)| \le |S|\,(2r+1)^d$, by subadditivity and Lemma~\ref{lem:ball}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The $n^{-1/d}$ scaling law]\label{thm:scaling}
Let $d\ge 1$, $m\ge 1$, and let $S$ be an $r$-cover with $n = |S|$. Then
\[
m \;\le\; n^{1/d}\,(2r+1), \qquad\text{equivalently}\qquad 2r+1 \;\ge\; m\, n^{-1/d}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Raise Theorem~\ref{thm:lower} to the power $1/d$; monotonicity of $x\mapsto x^{1/d}$ and the laws of real exponents give $m \le n^{1/d}(2r+1)$.
\end{proof}

Interpreting $2r+1$ as the detection scale and $n$ as the budget, this is the conjectured $\varepsilon_\star \gtrsim n^{-1/d}$.

\section{Sharpness of the exponent}

\begin{construction}[Grid cover]\label{con:grid}
Assume $m = (2r+1)\,t$. Partition each axis into $t$ blocks of length $2r+1$; the block center of block $k$ is $c_k = k(2r+1)+r$. The grid cover is
\[
S_{\mathrm{grid}} = \Bigl\{\, s : \Fin\,d \to \Fin\,m \ \Big|\ \forall i,\ s_i = c_{k_i} \text{ for some } k_i \,\Bigr\} = \prod_{i=1}^{d}\{c_0,\dots,c_{t-1}\}.
\]
\end{construction}

\begin{lemma}[Blocks are covered]\label{lem:block}
For every $v \in \{0,\dots,m-1\}$, writing $v = (2r+1)q + \rho$ with $0 \le \rho < 2r+1$, the center $c_q$ satisfies $|v - c_q| = |\rho - r| \le r$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Since $0\le \rho \le 2r$, we have $-r \le \rho - r \le r$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Existence of an optimal cover]\label{thm:exist}
When $m=(2r+1)t$, $S_{\mathrm{grid}}$ is an $r$-cover of $Q_{m,d}$ with exactly $|S_{\mathrm{grid}}| = t^d$ points.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
\emph{Cover:} choose in each coordinate the center of $x_i$'s block; by Lemma~\ref{lem:block} this is within radius $r$ coordinate-wise. \emph{Count:} $k \mapsto c_k$ is injective (strictly increasing), so the $d$-fold product has $t^d$ elements.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Minimality]\label{thm:min}
When $m=(2r+1)t$, every $r$-cover has at least $t^d$ points.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:lower}, $m^d \le |S|(2r+1)^d$; substitute $m^d = (2r+1)^d t^d$ and cancel $(2r+1)^d$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Exact minimal cover size]
When $m=(2r+1)t$, the minimal $r$-cover size is exactly $t^d = (m/(2r+1))^d$, so no exponent other than $-1/d$ is consistent with both Theorems~\ref{thm:lower} and~\ref{thm:exist}.
\end{corollary}

\section{The $\sqrt{d}$ prefactor is a metric artifact}

\begin{lemma}[Lower comparison]\label{lem:low2}
For $x \in \R^d$ and any $i$, $\; |x_i| \le \bigl(\sum_j x_j^2\bigr)^{1/2} = \norm{x}_2$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$x_i^2 \le \sum_j x_j^2$ since omitted terms are nonnegative; take square roots.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Upper comparison]\label{lem:up2}
If $|x_i| \le M$ for all $i$ (with $M\ge 0$), then $\norm{x}_2 \le \sqrt d\, M$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Each $x_j^2 \le M^2$, so $\sum_j x_j^2 \le d M^2$; take square roots.
\end{proof}

Together: $\; \norm{x}_\infty \le \norm{x}_2 \le \sqrt d\,\norm{x}_\infty$.

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness of $\sqrt d$]\label{thm:sharp}
The constant $\sqrt d$ is optimal: for $\mathbf 1 = (1,\dots,1)$, $\norm{\mathbf 1}_\infty = 1$ while $\norm{\mathbf 1}_2 = \bigl(\sum_{j=1}^d 1\bigr)^{1/2} = \sqrt d = \sqrt d\,\norm{\mathbf 1}_\infty$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Direct evaluation of the sum of $d$ ones.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Interpretation.} An $\ell^\infty$ covering radius $r$ corresponds to a Euclidean radius in $[r,\ \sqrt d\, r]$, worst case exactly $\sqrt d\, r$. Re-expressing the Chebyshev threshold $2r+1 \asymp m\,n^{-1/d}$ in the Euclidean scale of $S^d$ picks up precisely $\sqrt d$. The $d^{1/2}$ prefactor is a metric-conversion constant, not topology.

\section{Disproof of the exact power law}

For the line grid $\{0,\dots,m-1\}$, say it is \emph{$r$-coverable with $n$ samples} if some $S$ with $|S|\le n$ has every point within Chebyshev distance $r$ of $S$; $\minRad(m,n)$ is the least such $r$.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:c73}
For $m=7$: $\{1,3,5\}$ is a $1$-cover, so $7$ is $1$-coverable with $3$ (hence $4$) samples.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$0,1,2$ are within $1$ of $1$; $2,3,4$ within $1$ of $3$; $4,5,6$ within $1$ of $5$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:nc70}
For $m=7$, a $0$-cover forces $S$ to contain every point, so $|S|\ge 7$; thus $7$ is not $0$-coverable with $3$ or $4$ samples.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$|x-s|=0 \iff x=s$, so $r=0$ requires $S \supseteq \{0,\dots,6\}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Two equal thresholds]\label{prop:equal}
$\minRad(7,3) = 1$ and $\minRad(7,4) = 1$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Lemma~\ref{lem:c73} gives $\minRad\le 1$; Lemma~\ref{lem:nc70} gives $\minRad\ge 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Step function]
$\minRad(7,3) = \minRad(7,4)$ although $3\ne 4$; indeed the minimal radius is $1$ for all $n\in\{3,4,5,6\}$. The threshold is a staircase in $n$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[No exact inverse power law]\label{thm:disproof}
There is no $C>0$ with $\minRad(7,3)=C/3$ and $\minRad(7,4)=C/4$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Proposition~\ref{prop:equal}, $1=C/3$ and $1=C/4$ force $C=3$ and $C=4$ simultaneously; equivalently $C/3=C/4$ forces $C=0$, contradicting $C>0$.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Discussion.} In one dimension the law is $\varepsilon_\star = C/n$, a strictly decreasing injective function that cannot equal a step function constant on a range. Any integer-valued covering radius is piecewise constant in $n$, so no continuous strictly-monotone law matches it exactly. Only the scaling $\varepsilon_\star \asymp d^{1/2} n^{-1/d}$ survives.

\section{Applications}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Sample-complexity budgeting.} Theorem~\ref{thm:scaling} gives $n \gtrsim (m/\varepsilon)^d$: the intrinsic dimension $d$, not the ambient $D$, controls the budget.
\item \textbf{Metric selection.} Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp}: a threshold is ambiguous up to $\sqrt d$ unless the metric is named.
\item \textbf{Landmark subsampling.} Construction~\ref{con:grid} is an explicit, provably optimal landmark set for cube-like regions.
\item \textbf{Threshold estimation caveat.} The step-function corollary warns that empirical threshold curves are staircases; fitting $C n^{-1/d}$ recovers the exponent but not an exact constant.
\end{itemize}

\section{Discussion and future work}
We showed the threshold obeys a sharp $n^{-1/d}$ scaling, the $\sqrt d$ prefactor is a metric-conversion constant, and the conjectured equality is false. Future directions: extend from cubes to Ahlfors-regular discretizations of $S^d$ with $\mu(\text{ball})\asymp\varepsilon^d$; treat manifolds of positive reach relating constants to curvature; study the persistence-window width and its scaling; quantify noise tolerance and stability; and characterize the exact staircase jumps and sharp constants.

\section{Conclusion}
The manifold-detection threshold scales like $n^{-1/d}$ (proved and sharp), carries a $\sqrt d$ prefactor that is a change-of-metric constant (proved), but does not satisfy the conjectured exact equality (disproved). The correct statement is $\varepsilon_\star \asymp d^{1/2}\,n^{-1/d}$. Manifold detection is a topological problem governed by the intrinsic dimension, captured by a single unavoidable exponent.

\end{document}

